19. Understanding the Importance of Feature Selection

Another important learning was that the features selected for clustering define what "similar customers" means.

Adding or removing a feature can change the resulting segments.

Therefore:

Relevant features can help create meaningful segments.
Irrelevant features can introduce noise.
Poor-quality features can produce poor clusters.
Business understanding is important when selecting clustering variables.

I learned that there is no universal definition of a "similar customer." It depends on which characteristics are included in the analysis.

20. Understanding the Role of K in K-Means

The exercise used:

K = 4

which means four clusters were requested.

I learned that choosing the number of clusters is not always purely mathematical.

The elbow method can provide guidance, but the final number should also consider business requirements.

For example, if Marketing can realistically run only a few different campaigns, creating a very large number of segments may not be useful.

Therefore, the number of clusters should be both statistically reasonable and practically actionable.

21. Understanding Unsupervised Learning in Business

This exercise showed me how unsupervised learning can be useful when a business does not already have predefined customer categories.

Instead of telling the algorithm:

"This customer is a Heavy-Data Youth."

we provide customer behaviour and allow the algorithm to discover groups.

The business can then study those groups and decide what they represent.

This makes clustering useful for discovering customer patterns that may not have been identified using manually created rules.

22. From Discovery to Operationalisation

One of the most important concepts at the end of the exercise was the difference between discovery and operationalisation.

Stage 1 — Discover

K-Means is used to discover customer segments.

Customer Behaviour
        ↓
K-Means
        ↓
Customer Segments

This is unsupervised learning.

Stage 2 — Operationalise

Once the segments have been discovered and named, a supervised classifier can be trained to assign new customers to those segments.

Discovered Segments
        ↓
Training Labels
        ↓
Classifier
        ↓
New Subscriber Segment

This is supervised learning.

The key concept I learned was:

Unsupervised learning discovers the structure, while supervised learning operationalises it.

