Key Takeaway

The main takeaway from this exercise was that K-Means can discover customer groups without being given predefined labels, but the quality of those groups depends heavily on the features, scaling, and business interpretation.

I learned that:

K-Means is an unsupervised learning technique.
It groups customers based on similarity and distance.
Feature scaling is important when variables have different ranges.
Large numerical values can dominate distance-based algorithms.
Cluster numbers such as 0, 1, 2, and 3 have no business meaning by themselves.
The business team must interpret and name the segments.
There is no traditional accuracy score for an unsupervised clustering task.
A useful segmentation should be meaningful and actionable.
The choice of features affects the definition of customer similarity.
K-Means can be used to discover customer personas that were not provided to the model.
Discovered segments can later be operationalised using supervised learning.

Overall, this exercise helped me understand how unsupervised learning can be used to discover hidden customer structures and convert them into actionable marketing segments.